          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What does the diagnosis mean, what does guidance describe as first-line therapy, and which code covers it?",
          "answer": "**What the diagnosis means.** Type 2 diabetes is a problem with how the body manages blood sugar (glucose). The body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, a state called insulin resistance, and the pancreas cannot make enough extra insulin to compensate, so blood glucose stays above the normal range. It develops gradually over years, and many people have no symptoms when it is found. Diet guidance notes that a sustained weight reduction of 5 percent or more improves glucose control, and that substantial weight loss can put the condition into remission in some people. Remission means normal glucose without glucose-lowering medication. It is not a cure, and monitoring continues. (Sources: *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*; *Diet for type 2 diabetes*.)\n\n**First-line treatment.** The sources disagree on what I could find. One search did not retrieve any description of first-line drug treatment. A second search found the national diabetes association standards of care, which say the first choice depends on other health conditions you may have. Where no other condition changes the choice, metformin is described as the usual first medicine, taken alongside a structured lifestyle programme, with kidney function checked before starting and periodically afterwards. I could not retrieve the guidance on which medicines are preferred when other conditions are present. Your clinician can tell you what applies to you.\n\n**The code.** Type 2 diabetes is coded **E11** in ICD-10-CM, Chapter 4 (endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases). The chapter sorts diabetes by its cause, not its treatment, so a person on insulin is still coded E11, and the insulin use is recorded separately. A record that says only \"diabetes mellitus\" with no type defaults to type 2. One search reported that its code lookup failed, so please check the code against the paperwork from your diagnosis. A code is a record-keeping label and says nothing about your treatment or prognosis.\n\n**Where guidance differs, and what I could not answer.** Diabetes association guidance describes screening all adults from age 35, while preventive services guidance limits routine screening to adults aged 35 to 70 who are overweight or obese. The diabetes association describes an HbA1c below 7 percent as a reasonable goal for many adults, and below 6.5 percent for some people, but I could not retrieve the passage explaining where the sources disagree on this target. I also could not retrieve a plain definition of the diagnosis or the numbers that define diabetes. The material I found covers only the prediabetes range (HbA1c 5.7 to 6.4 percent) and says that when two tests disagree, the usual approach is to repeat the abnormal one. Please take your questions about medicines for your other conditions and your personal blood sugar target to your clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
